DESCRIPTIONS = json.loads('{"Start Here": "the mental models, expectations, and learning sequence that make later TypeScript topics easier to reason about", "Setup and Tooling": "installing, running, configuring, and debugging the TypeScript toolchain", "Compiler Fundamentals": "how TypeScript parses source, builds symbols, checks types, reports diagnostics, and optionally emits output", "JavaScript Foundations for TypeScript": "the JavaScript runtime behavior that TypeScript models rather than replaces", "Type System Foundations": "the relationships TypeScript uses to infer, compare, narrow, and assign types", "Primitive Types": "the TypeScript representations of JavaScript primitive values and special control-flow types", "Literal Types": "types that represent exact values and preserve domain-specific information", "Variables and Inference": "how declarations, mutability, annotations, and widening influence inferred types", "Arrays and Tuples": "typed ordered collections, fixed-position records, and variadic argument lists", "Object Types": "structural descriptions of object properties, keys, mutability, and exactness limitations", "Functions": "typing callable behavior, parameters, returns, callbacks, overloads, generics, and variance", "Union and Intersection Types": "combining alternatives and constraints to model domain variants safely", "Type Narrowing": "using runtime evidence and control flow to refine broad static types", "Type Aliases and Interfaces": "naming, extending, composing, and publishing reusable structural contracts", "Enums and Alternatives": "runtime enum objects and lighter JavaScript-first ways to represent finite choices", "Classes": "typing JavaScript classes, construction, inheritance, visibility, and instance behavior", "Generics": "preserving relationships between input and output types without giving up reuse", "keyof and Indexed Access": "deriving keys and property types from existing object and tuple types", "typeof and Value-to-Type Patterns": "deriving static types from runtime declarations to maintain one source of truth", "Mapped Types": "systematically transforming every property in an object-like type", "Conditional Types": "selecting types according to assignability relationships", "infer Keyword": "extracting component types from larger type patterns inside conditional types", "Template Literal Types": "building and parsing string-shaped types from literal unions", "Utility Types": "standard reusable type transformations included with TypeScript", "Type Assertions": "overriding the checker when the programmer has evidence it cannot derive", "satisfies Operator": "checking a value against a contract while preserving its useful inferred type", "Null and Undefined": "modeling absence explicitly under strict null checking", "Error Handling": "representing thrown errors and expected failure paths without losing safety", "Async TypeScript": "typing promises, concurrency, cancellation, and asynchronous iteration", "Iterators and Generators": "typing pull-based sequences and functions that pause and resume", "Modules": "aligning TypeScript imports and exports with the runtime and build tool", "Declaration Files": "describing JavaScript APIs for the checker without emitting runtime code", "Libraries and Type Definitions": "selecting, resolving, and publishing platform and package type declarations", "tsconfig.json": "defining the compiler program, runtime assumptions, emit policy, and project boundaries", "Strictness Options": "turning uncertain JavaScript behavior into explicit, actionable diagnostics", "Project References": "splitting large repositories into independently buildable typed projects", "TypeScript with Node.js": "matching TypeScript configuration and types to modern Node.js runtime semantics", "TypeScript with React": "typing components, hooks, events, refs, and reusable component APIs", "TypeScript with Vue": "typing Vue macros, templates, composables, state, and generic components", "Runtime Validation": "converting untrusted runtime data into validated values with trustworthy static types", "API Design": "designing stable request, response, error, pagination, and generated contracts", "Database Types": "separating storage records, domain models, commands, and public DTOs", "Advanced Type Patterns": "encoding stronger domain invariants with reusable type-level patterns", "Variance": "understanding how subtyping behaves through generic containers and callable parameters", "Type-Level Programming": "treating types as compile-time transformations while managing complexity limits", "Library Authoring": "shipping stable public types, declarations, module formats, and package metadata", "Type Te